```apex
/**
 * Asynchronously creates a CSAT survey Task for a single Case.
 * Enqueued once per newly-closed case so that one failure
 * does not block the remaining cases.
 */
public class CaseSurveyJob implements System.Queueable {

    private final Id caseId;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseId = caseId;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        // Re-query to be safe: CaseNumber may not be in the in-memory
        // record if the caller used a partial field set (e.g. API).
        List<Case> cases = new List<Case>();
        cases.add([
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id = :caseId
            LIMIT 1
        ]);

        Case c = cases[0];

        // Guard: skip if Contact was removed after the trigger fired
        if (c.ContactId == null) return;

        Task surveyTask = new Task();
        surveyTask.WhatId       = c.Id;
        surveyTask.WhoId        = c.ContactId;
        surveyTask.Subject      = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber;
        surveyTask.ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2);
        surveyTask.Status       = 'Not Started';

        insert surveyTask;
    }
}
```

### Notes / things I changed and why

| Decision | Reason |
|---|---|
| **Collect IDs first, enqueue second** | Keeps the filter/transition logic in a clean pass and makes the 50-job boundary a single numeric loop instead of scattered `if` checks. |
| **Chunked enqueue loop (`i += 50`)** | Salesforce throws `Exception: Maximum number of queue jobs has been exceeded` at 51. The chunk loop lets the trigger handle up to 200 (or any multiple of 50) cleanly within a single transaction. For truly unbounded volumes you would layer a dispatcher Queueable, but the prompt's "one job per case" contract is preserved. |
| **`!old.IsClosed` check** | Ensures we only fire on a *transition*, not on every edit of an already-closed case (e.g., a note added to a closed case). |
| **Re-query `CaseNumber` inside the job** | External API callers (the nightly cleanup) may only update a subset of fields in the DML, so `CaseNumber` might be absent from the in-memory `Trigger.new`. Querying in the async execution guarantees we get the real value. |
| **No `Database.AllowsCallouts`** | The job only inserts a `Task`—no HTTP callouts needed yet. Removing the interface avoids unnecessary governor-overhead and keeps the class minimal for the future vendor-integration change. |
| **`ContactId == null` guard inside the job** | Defensive: if the contact is disassociated between the trigger transaction and the queueable execution (a rare but possible race in high-concurrency orgs), we skip cleanly instead of throwing on `WhoId`. |

